The DistributedLocking idea involving stealing locks from other machines can be applied to single machines with multiple cores.

In this case the localLock.lock/unlock will be noops (wow saves us CAS + fences + possible spin/sleep time right?). Yes because
a cpu core can claim a lock if another core contends for it then it will queue a per core RPC that will change per core ownership
of this thread. When a lock ownership changes we can add a full memory barrier.

NOTE: This wont be easy, we will have to add per cpu RCP feature in the kernel. Something like:
      perCoreRPC( coreID, functionPtr )


Q: What happens if a thread runs on core 0 acquires lock midway through its critical section switches out.
   Next time it gets loaded its on a different core. 

A: Well technically it will behave as if it has the lock and I suppose your question is what happens while its running
   if another thread now runs on core 0, it will proceed and both threads will be in at the same time right?


Q: YEP!

A: We could solve that with a per cpu isLockHeld variable, its not volatile and not atomic. So other threads should not 
   enter core 0.


Q: What happens when this bastard thread completes?

A: We will have to ask it to write isLockHeld = false and accompany that with a storestore barrier.


Q: Hmm this is getting complicated, are we sure context switches come with the right kind of barriers 
   on all CPUs?

A: No, I m not sure so yes point taken, this theory is contingent upon the right barriers taking place when context
   switch takes place.